  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY NIGHT THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 205 PM CDT Mon Aug 17 2026\n\nThe storm system that moves across the area Tuesday into \nTuesday night will usher in a pattern change to northwesterly \nflow across the Upper Midwest with ridging and a heat dome to \nour west and a trough over the eastern US. This places Iowa near\nthe main storm track and in the transition zone to warmer \ntemperatures to our southwest. This flow will lead to shortwave \nenergy moving across the area every 36 to 48 hours with \nscattered showers and storms possible with each passing \nshortwave. Rain amounts are expected to be light with NBM \nprobabilities of 0.5 inches or more below 10% through the \nperiod. \n\nTuesday night, a cool front is forecast to move southeastward across \nthe area through 12 UTC Wednesday. The better lift is forecast to \noverspread the area after 00 UTC Wednesday. There is some \ndisagreement between the different models on the amount of \ninstability available after 00 UTC but 0 to 6km shear of 30 to 40 \nknots will be in place with the NBM mean CAPE of 1000 to 1500 \nJ/KG of CAPE. There is also a similar signal in the HREF. \nRainfall amounts will be light with most locations receiving \nless than a quarter of an inch. NBM probabilities of greater \nthan a quarter of an inch are 10 to 15%. High pressure is \nforecast to quickly build into the area at the surface and \naloft. This will bring quiet weather to the area",
